{
  "claims": [
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "In the observational, prospective, single-arm, single-center DYNAMITE cohort study, which enrolled 55 patients (55 de novo complex coronary lesions) treated with the DynamX sirolimus-eluting bioadaptor, the primary co-endpoints were the difference in mean device area and mean in-device lumen area from postprocedure to 9 months as measured by optical coherence tomography; postprocedural mean device area was 8.15 ± 0.64 mm² and in-device lumen area 8.27 ± 1.78 mm², and at 9-month follow-up mean device area increased to 8.53 ± 1.71 mm² (absolute difference 0.37 ± 0.99 mm²; relative difference 5.52 ± 14.8%, p = 0.010) while mean in-device lumen area decreased to 7.57 ± 1.86 mm² (absolute difference -0.70 ± 1.09 mm²; relative difference -7.98 ± 14.9%, p < 0.001).",
      "cite": ["PMID:41765253"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E5", "E6", "E7", "E10", "E11"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "At 24-month follow-up after DynamX implantation in the DYNAMITE study, 4 patients (7.4%) experienced major adverse cardiac events, including one target vessel myocardial infarction and 4 target lesion revascularization events.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41765253"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E12"]
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 0.95,
  "evidence_status": "complete",
  "evidence_gap": ""
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "In a cross-sectional pilot study of matched veterans with and without HIV at the Providence VA Medical Center, multivariable linear regressions were used to investigate associations between systemic inflammation, myocardial fibrosis and subclinical cardiac disease; the analysis included 21 veterans with HIV (mean age 54 years; 71% White, 29% Black) and 20 controls (mean age 56 years; 70% White, 30% Black). Higher growth differentiation factor (GDF)-15 blood levels and increased extracellular volume were found among the patients with HIV, suggesting the presence of increased myocardial fibrosis in this population, and limited data from this pilot suggest that HIV may be associated with increased cardiac fibrosis.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41791688"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E6", "E7", "E8"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "In the same pilot study of 21 veterans with HIV and 20 controls, patients with HIV showed lower right heart function on cardiac MRI, and the authors reported that limited data from this pilot suggest HIV may be associated with decreased right ventricular function.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41791688"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E7", "E8"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C3",
      "text": "The authors state that the study highlights the need for larger longitudinal studies to better understand the trajectory of HIV-associated cardiac changes and to help improve cardiac disease prevention in this population; because the reported pilot data are cross-sectional, they cannot describe that trajectory.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41791688"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E9"]
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 0.92,
  "evidence_status": "complete",
  "evidence_gap": ""
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "SCAR-Net was built as a deep learning model specifically designed for ultrasound-based discrimination between postoperative scar tissue and recurrent lesions in patients with breast cancer, developed using 34,376 ultrasound images from 5,710 patients across four hospitals and incorporating scar-recurrence feature enhancer and boundary-sensitive attention network modules.",
      "cite": ["PMID:42023157"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E3", "E4"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "SCAR-Net was validated in a multicenter setting, where it significantly improved radiologists' diagnostic performance in distinguishing postoperative scars from recurrent lesions; the model also demonstrated superior segmentation accuracy with Dice coefficients of 0.918-0.925 compared with traditional methods (0.795-0.811).",
      "cite": ["PMID:42023157"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E5", "E6"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C3",
      "text": "In multicenter validation, SCAR-Net increased radiologists' AUC from 0.810-0.824 to 0.939-0.942, sensitivity from 0.775-0.782 to 0.934-0.941, and specificity from 0.839-0.872 to 0.935-0.950 (all p < 0.001).",
      "cite": ["PMID:42023157"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E5"]
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 0.9,
  "evidence_status": "complete",
  "evidence_gap": ""
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "In preclinical evaluation of LGR5-targeting radiotracers, saturation binding assays showed that [68Ga]Ga-LTP-02 exhibited higher binding affinity for LGR5 (Kd = 74.11 ± 24.52 nM) than [68Ga]Ga-LTP-01 (Kd = 153.40 ± 17.14 nM).",
      "cite": ["PMID:42000453"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E5"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "Dynamic PET imaging revealed considerably higher uptake of [68Ga]Ga-LTP-02 in LGR5-positive A549 xenografts (4.74 ± 0.44 %ID/mL) than in LGR5-negative NCI-H1299 xenografts (0.98 ± 0.08 %ID/mL), and consistently the tumor-to-muscle ratio was markedly higher in the A549 xenograft model (4.07 ± 0.07) than in the NCI-H1299 model (0.77 ± 0.10).",
      "cite": ["PMID:42000453"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E6", "E7"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "Biodistribution studies further confirmed that [68Ga]Ga-LTP-02 can specifically target LGR5-positive tumors.",
      "cite": ["PMID:42000453"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E8"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "For the Nectin4-targeting bicyclic peptide probes [68Ga]Ga-NOTA-DN96 and [18F]AlF-NOTA-DN96, cellular uptake assays demonstrated markedly higher accumulation of both tracers in MC38-Nectin4 cells (Nectin4+) compared with MC38 cells (Nectin4-negative, Nectin4-).",
      "cite": ["PMID:41916034"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E20"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "In agreement with these results, biodistribution studies and PET imaging revealed pronounced and specific tracer accumulation in MC38-Nectin4 tumor models, whereas uptake in MC38 tumors remained substantially lower; the tumor-to-muscle (T/M) ratio increased progressively over time and reached its maximum at 120 min post-injection (p.i.).",
      "cite": ["PMID:41916034"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E21", "E22"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "Tumor uptake of [68Ga]Ga-NOTA-DN96 was consistently higher than that of [18F]AlF-NOTA-DN96 across all tumor models examined.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41916034"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E23"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "Collectively, [68Ga]Ga-/[18F]AlF-NOTA-DN96 demonstrated excellent stability, high target specificity, and partial hepatobiliary elimination, highlighting their potential as promising PET probes for imaging Nectin4-expressing tumors.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41916034"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E25"]
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 0.9,
  "evidence_status": "complete",
  "evidence_gap": ""
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "In a simulation study using data from an international AI confirmatory study (February 2022–November 2023), MRI examinations of 500 men with suspected clinically significant prostate cancer (csPCa) from four European centers were included; AI-triaging thresholds were calibrated on 100 examinations and the workflow was simulated on 400 examinations, including examinations from an external site, incorporating assessments from 62 radiologists.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41960994"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E3", "E4", "E6", "E8"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "Among the 400 patients (median age, 66 years; IQR, 60–69 years) included for testing, radiologists achieved a sensitivity of 89.4% (95% CI: 85.8, 93.1) and specificity of 57.7% (95% CI: 52.3, 63.1), whereas the AI-driven pathway maintained comparable sensitivity (89.0%; 95% CI: 85.0, 93.0; P = .36) but improved specificity by 11.5%, reaching 69.2% (95% CI: 64.4, 74.0; P < .001).",
      "cite": ["PMID:41960994"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E11", "E12"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "Within the simulated workflow, the AI system triaged and diagnosed 195 of 400 (49%; 95% CI: 173, 216) examinations with sensitivity of 94.7% (95% CI: 89.5, 99.9) and specificity of 94.7% (95% CI: 90.5, 98.9), and the study concluded that triaging by this AI system improved simulated diagnostic workflow efficiency without compromising diagnostic accuracy for csPCa.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41960994"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E13", "E14"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "SCAR-Net, a deep learning model designed for ultrasound-based discrimination between postoperative scar tissue and recurrent lesions in breast cancer, was developed using 34,376 ultrasound images from 5,710 patients across four hospitals and incorporated scar-recurrence feature enhancer and boundary-sensitive attention network modules.",
      "cite": ["PMID:42023157"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E19", "E20"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "In multicenter validation on patient ultrasound images, SCAR-Net significantly improved radiologists' diagnostic performance, increasing AUC from 0.810–0.824 to 0.939–0.942, sensitivity from 0.775–0.782 to 0.934–0.941, and specificity from 0.839–0.872 to 0.935–0.950 (all p < 0.001).",
      "cite": ["PMID:42023157"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E21"]
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 0.95,
  "evidence_status": "complete",
  "evidence_gap": ""
}

{
  "claims": [],
  "confidence": 0.9,
  "evidence_status": "insufficient",
  "evidence_gap": "The supplied study (PMID:41960994) is a retrospective simulation of an AI triaging workflow and reports only simulated diagnostic performance of the AI-driven pathway versus the conventional radiologist workflow among 400 test examinations (comparable sensitivity 89.0% vs 89.4%, improved specificity 69.2% vs 57.7%). It does not report biopsy rates, unnecessary biopsies, or any patient outcome (e.g., morbidity, mortality, or clinical management consequences) for AI triage versus a radiologist-only workflow, so the requested claim that deploying AI triage reduces unnecessary biopsies or improves patient outcomes versus a radiologist-only workflow is not supported."
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "For fMRI, traditional MRI methodologies require full k-space information following a unique data acquisition sequence to reconstruct the complete image, a process the authors describe as a time-consuming obstacle for medical imaging techniques; to address this, the study proposes a novel image reconstruction method intended to enhance the efficiency of data acquisition while maintaining high accuracy in activation detection, combining through-plane and in-plane acceleration techniques to accelerate image acquisition along two dimensions and using multiple image-shift strategies together with a 2D Hadamard encoding scheme to increase encoding diversity and reduce slice leakage.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41951145"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E4", "E5", "E6", "E7", "E8"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "For MR image denoising, the reported problem is that the inherent correlation between Rice's noise and the Magnetic Resonance (MR) signal during MR image formation makes denoising important but complex, and it can prove insufficient and even result in loss of information details; although filtering methods based on Partial Differential Equations (PDEs) were introduced to overcome these drawbacks, their proposed filter parameters must be determined with great accuracy. The authors' approach is to propose adapted filtering anisotropic diffusion PDE filter models using precise local parameters adjustment in order to denoise MR images with high Rice's noise levels; these filtering models incorporate a robust Rician noise estimator and an unbiased variant of filters based on anisotropic diffusion, whose parameters are all calculated simultaneously and optimized adaptively through the introduction of the PSO algorithm and the use of reference-based and no-reference quality metrics (PSNR, SSIM and Blind/Referenceless Image Spatial Quality Evaluator (BRISQUE)), yielding three unbiased anisotropic diffusion filtering models with optimized parameters for Rician noise removal in MR images.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41903663"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E12", "E13", "E15", "E16", "E11"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C3",
      "text": "By applying the approach to both simulated and experimental fMRI data, the authors report reducing total scan time while achieving a higher signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) and contrast-to-noise ratio (CNR) in regions of interest (ROI), and compared with conventional reconstruction strategies the proposed method demonstrates potential improvements in activation detection under specific acceleration and encoding conditions, while also providing voxel-wise estimates through a Bayesian framework.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41951145"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E9", "E10"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C4",
      "text": "The various tests performed on synthetic and real MR images provided excellent results compared to some other published methods, demonstrating the high performance of the proposed filters in terms of noise reduction and preservation of important details.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41903663"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E17"]
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 0.9,
  "evidence_status": "complete",
  "evidence_gap": ""
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "In a multicenter cross-sectional study of 241 hemodialysis patients who underwent both transthoracic echocardiography and AV access flow measurement, left ventricular mass index (LVMI) was used as a surrogate marker of cardiovascular morbidity, and AV access flow was quantified as brachial artery flow volume (FV) by pulsed Doppler ultrasonography (mean FV 0.71 ± 0.29 L/min; median dialysis vintage 5.0 years, interquartile range 3.0–11.0 years).",
      "cite": ["PMID:41644509"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E4", "E5", "E6", "E8", "E9"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "Multivariate regression models were applied to investigate the associations of FV and dialysis vintage with LVMI: higher FV was significantly associated with increased LVMI (mean difference = 22.83; 95% confidence interval [CI]: 0.37 to 45.29; p = 0.046), whereas dialysis vintage was not (mean difference = 0.51; 95% CI: -0.41 to 1.45; p = 0.27), and no statistically significant interaction was observed (mean difference = 0.13; 95% CI: -3.14 to 3.40; p = 0.94).",
      "cite": ["PMID:41644509"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E7", "E10", "E11"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "Because the study was a multicenter cross-sectional design that enrolled hemodialysis patients undergoing both transthoracic echocardiography and AV access flow measurement between April 2014 and January 2024, it can document an association between AV access flow and LVMI but cannot establish that lowering access flow would improve cardiac outcomes.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41644509"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E4"]
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 0.9,
  "evidence_status": "complete",
  "evidence_gap": ""
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "In the retrospective monocentric cohort of 3455 women with singleton pregnancies who delivered vaginally in 2023 at the Fondazione IRCCS Ca' Granda Ospedale Maggiore Policlinico in Milan (3090 spontaneous vaginal births and 365 assisted vaginal births), multivariable logistic regression was used to identify factors independently associated with assisted vaginal birth.",
      "cite": ["PMID:42036403"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E4", "E5", "E7"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "After adjusting for all factors considered, maternal age (OR 1.04, 95% CI: 1.01-1.07), mode of conception (OR 1.58, 95% CI: 1.07-2.33), epidural analgesia (OR 6.25, 95% CI: 3.05-12.80), gestational age (OR 1.48, 95% CI: 1.31-1.67), and newborn male sex (OR 1.35, 95% CI: 1.06-1.73) were positively associated with assisted vaginal birth, whereas parity (OR 0.20, 95% CI: 0.14-0.29) was inversely associated.",
      "cite": ["PMID:42036403"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E8"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "A secondary analysis of the same retrospective cohort compared maternal and neonatal outcomes between perineal and mid/low cavity vacuum applications.",
      "cite": ["PMID:42036403"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E6"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "Compared to perineal applications, mid/low pelvic vacuum applications were associated with greater blood loss (p < 0.01), a higher episiotomy rate (95.5% vs 88.2%, p = 0.03), and increased ultrasound use (62.4% vs 23.5%, p < 0.01).",
      "cite": ["PMID:42036403"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E9"]
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 0.9,
  "evidence_status": "complete",
  "evidence_gap": ""
}

{
  "claims": [],
  "confidence": 0.9,
  "evidence_status": "insufficient",
  "evidence_gap": "The retrieved ASRT study (PMID:42058324) is a single-cohort prospective series: 41 lung patients were enrolled and received 232 fractions, of which 178 (76.7%) were treated with ASRT, and outcomes are reported for the whole cohort without a nonadaptive thoracic radiotherapy comparison group. Survival outcomes were assessed by Kaplan-Meier analysis, and at a median follow-up of 19.8 months 1-year local control, disease-free survival and overall survival for the whole cohort were 94%, 80% and 84% respectively; no comparative data versus nonadaptive thoracic radiotherapy are reported. Therefore the requested comparison — whether adaptive skin radiotherapy improves local control or survival relative to nonadaptive thoracic radiotherapy — is not supported by the retrieved evidence."
